Using Code BlocksUsing the Graphical Editor <p>Create a code block in your markdown document and identify it with <code>tikz</code>. You can include packages like <code>pgfplots</code> or libraries in the preamble:</p> <pre><code>\usepackage{pgfplots}
\begin{document}
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \begin{axis}[xlabel=$x$, ylabel=$y$]
    \addplot[color=red,mark=x] coordinates {
      (2,-3) (3,5) (4,7)
    };
  \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{document}
</code></pre> <ol> <li>Click the Edit visually button on any rendered TikZ SVG block, or use the Command Palette (Ctrl+P → <code>Insert display math</code> or search for the TikZ Editor commands).</li> <li>A CAD-style modal opens:<ul> <li>Left Sidebar: Component library featuring core shapes (lines, circles, rectangles) and package modules.</li> <li>Center Canvas: Interactive grid with zoom (Ctrl+Scroll Lock), panning (++spacebar+drag++), and snapping (toggle via G or H).</li> <li>Right Sidebar: Property editor (adjust color, bold, italics, font size, stroke thickness) and raw TikZ Code synchronization.</li> </ul> </li> <li>Press Ctrl+Z to undo and Ctrl+Y to redo actions. </li> <li>Click the Update block button to compile and insert the code block into your note.</li> </ol>
